WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.090
Compression squeezes and shortens rock, tension stretches it, and shear makes

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.550
adjacent material move past itself.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:11.870
The same stress category can produce different structures under different conditions.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.990
In the shallow crust, compression can form faults;

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.850
deeper, hot rocks can fold or flow.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:20.830
A brittle rock breaks or slips when stressed.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.190
At depth, higher confinement and temperature commonly favor ductile behavior:

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.190
minerals and rock masses change shape without a throughgoing fracture.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.870
Ductile does not mean liquid, and the transition depends on the

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:33.790
material and conditions.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.970
At increasing depth, minerals may deform, grow, or react to form

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.150
new minerals stable under changed conditions.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.110
Water in pore spaces opposes part of the confining pressure and

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.930
can assist fracture or reactions.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.050
Fluid effects depend on location and conditions rather than one universal

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:51.490
outcome.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:57.070
Geologists investigate mineral stability and rock behavior using controlled temperature and

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.730
pressure experiments, field observations, and measurements of natural rocks.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.350
Each line of evidence has a defined range of conditions.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.950
A single specimen cannot establish the strength of every rock at

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:07.550
a given depth.
